      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A gas odor or any carbon monoxide reading means leaving the building first and calling 911 or the gas utility from outside. Nobody should restart, open or inspect the equipment in that situation. Soot around the burners belongs in the same group. In buildings with flats above a storefront, the Illinois Carbon Monoxide Alarm Detector Act sets the alarm requirement for those dwelling units. A second group is mechanical: a cracked heat exchanger, repeated breaker trips, scorched contactors and a short-cycling compressor. Equipment in that condition goes off and stays off until a technician has it open. City & Suburban offers same-day service when the schedule allows for failures in that second group."
